One thing to notice in the output

User 2's list has several high-numbered, obscure-looking IDs: 158966, 178827, 128520, 117192. In MovieLens, high IDs are mostly newer, lesser-known movies. This is the weakness I flagged earlier. A movie only needs 2 neighbors to qualify, and if both rated it far above their own averages, its predicted score shoots up. Two enthusiastic votes on a movie almost nobody has seen can beat a well-established favourite.

You can check this yourself in PowerShell, from compute/. Replace the ID with any one from the list:

powershell
Select-String "^178827," ..\data\movies.csv
(Select-String ",178827," ..\data\ratings.csv).Count

The first line shows the title. The second shows how many people rated it. If that number is tiny, like 2 to 5, you've seen the problem directly.

Is it a bug?

No. The algorithm is doing exactly what it was designed to do, and your tests prove that. It's a tuning question: how much evidence should we ask for before trusting a prediction? Two standard remedies:

Raise minNeighborsPerMovie from 2 to something like 3 to 5. It's the simplest option, and it costs some coverage.
Shrink similarity when the overlap is small, so neighbors who share only 5 movies with the user count for less than neighbors who share 50.

I haven't changed either, because choosing between them by eye would be guessing. That's the job of the hold-out evaluation you parked: it measures which setting predicts hidden ratings best. Add a line to your todolater file so you don't forget: "run Evaluator, tune minNeighborsPerMovie and similarity shrinkage."

